===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|srew}}
From {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|reume}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|rheuma}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|ῥεῦμα||stream, humour|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɹuːm/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːm|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{context|uncountable|lang=en}} Watery or thin discharge of [[serum]] or [[mucus]], especially from the [[eye]]s or [[nose]], formerly thought to cause disease. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1916''', {{w|James Joyce}}, ''A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man'', Macmillan Press Ltd, 102
#*: He wore about his shoulders a heavy cloak; his pale face was drawn and his voice broken with '''rheum'''.
# [[illness|Illness]] or [[disease]] thought to be caused by such secretions; a [[cold]], [[catarrh]]; [[rheumatism]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Flr Mntgn Essays}}, II.8:
#*: he{{...}}had all his faculties free and easie, onely a '''rheume''' excepted that fell into his stomacke.
# {{context|poetic|lang=en}} [[tears|Tears]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}

